  "grammar": {
    "title": "Can, can't, and could",
    "explanation": "Use **can** to talk about what you are able to do now. I **can** pay with cash.\n\nUse **can't** when you are not able to do it. I **can't** buy an expensive car.\n\nUse **could** to talk about the past. Yesterday I **could** buy cheap bread for one coin.\n\n**Can**, **can't**, and **could** never change. We say I **can**, you **can**, she **can** — no -s!",
    "examples": [
      "I *can* pay with cash. (able now)",
      "She *can* buy cheap bread. (able now)",
      "I *can't* buy an expensive car. (not able)",
      "He *can't* sell his old bike. (not able)",
      "I *could* buy bread for one coin when I was little. (past)",
      "We *could* pay with cash yesterday. (past)"
    ],
    "practice": [
      {
        "q": "Can you buy bread?",
        "a": "Yes, I can. / No, I can't."
      },
      {
        "q": "Could you pay with cash yesterday?",
        "a": "Yes, I could. / No, I couldn't."
      }
    ],
